This chapter covers bash concepts that can make your scripts more intelligent. You'll learn how to test conditions, use loops, consolidaate code into fucntiosn, send commands to the background and more. 
You'll also learn some ways of customizing your bash environment for penetration testing. 

Test Operators 

  Bash lests us selectively execute commands when certain conditions are met. 
  We can use test operators to craft a wide variety of conditions, such as whether one value equals another value, whether a file is of a certaintypem or whether one value is greater than another. 
  We often rely such tests to determine whether to continue running a block of code, so being able to construct them is fundamental to bash programming. 

  Bash has multiple kinds of test operators. (Page 28). 
  File test operators allow us to perform tests against files on the filesystem, such as checking whether a file is executable or whether a certain directory exists. 


File Test Operators
Operator Description 
-d       Checks whether the file is a directory
-r       Checks whether the file is readable 
-x       Checks whether the file is executable 
-w       Checks whether the file is writable 
-f       Checks whether the file is a regular file
-s       Checks whether the file is greater than zero

String Comparison Operators 
Operator Description
=        Checks whether the string is equal to another string
==       Synonym of = when used within [[]] constructs
!=       Checks whether a string is not equal to another string
<        Checks whether a string comes before another string (in alphabetical order)
>        Checks whether a string comes after another string (in alphabetical order)
-z       Checks whether a string is null
-n       Checks whether a string is not null

Integer comparison operators allows us to perform checks on integers, such as whether an integer is less than or greater than another. 


Integer Comparison Operatores
Operator Description 
-eq      Checks whether a number is equal to another
-ne      Checks whether a number is not equal to another
-ge      Checks whether a number is greater than or equal to another
-gt      Checks whether a number is greater than another number
-lt      Checks whether a number is less than another number
-le      Checks whether a number is less than or equal to another number

if Conditions 

  In bash, we can use the if condition to execute code only when a certain condition is met. 

if [[ condition ]]; then
  # Do something if the condition is met
else 
  # Do something if the condition is not met
fi

  We start with the if keyword, followed by a test condition between double square brackets ([[]]).
  We then use the ; character to separate the if keyword from the then keyword, which allows us to introduce a block of code that runs only if the condition is met.
  Next, we use the else keyword to introduce a fallback code block that runs if the condition is not met. 
  Note that else is optional, and you may not always need it. 
  Finally, we close the if condition with the fi keyword(which is if reversed). 

  Note: In some operating systems, such as those often used in containers, the default shell might not neccessarily be bash. 
  To account for those cases, you may want to use single square brackets ([...]) rather than double to enclose your condition. 
  The use of single brackets meets Portable Operating System Interface standard and should work on almost any UNIX derivative, including Linux. (Page 29).

  Let's see an if condition in practice. 
  This if condition tests to see whether a file exists and, if not, creates it. 

test_if_file_exists.sh

#!/bin/bash 
FILENAME="flow_control_with_if.txt"

if [[ -f "${FILENAME}"]]; then 
  echo "${FILENAME} already exists."
  exit 1
else 
  touch "${FILENAME}"
fi

  We first create a variable named FILENAME containing the name of the file we need. 
  This saves from having to repeat the filename in the code. 
  We then introduce the if statement, which includes a condition that uses the -f file test operator to test for the existence of a file.
  If this condition is true, we use echo to printot teh screen a message explaining tha the file already exists and then use the status code 1 (failure) to exit the program. 
  In the else block, which will execute only if the file does not exist, we create the file by using the touch command. 

  Another way of achieving the same outcome is using the NOT operator (!) to check whether a directory doesn't exist and, if it doesn't creates it. 
  This example has fewer lines of code and eliminates the need for en else block altogether. 

#!/bin/bash 
FILENAME="flow_control_with_if.txt"

if [[ ! -f "${FILENAME}" ]]; then
  touch "${FILENAME}"
fi

  We can use if conditions with other kinds of test operators. 
  You can create a string comparison test. 
  It tests whether two variables are by performing string comparison with the equal-to operator (==). 

string_comparison.sh

#!/bin/bash
VARIABLE_ONE="nostarch"
VARIABLE_TWO="nostarch"

if [[ "${VARIABLE_ONE}" = "${VARIABLE_TWO}" ]]; then 
  echo "They are equal!"
else
  echo "They are not equal!"
fi

  The script will compare the two variables, both of which have the value nostarch, and print They are equal! by using the echo command.
  Next, is an integer comparison test, which takes two integers and checks which one is the larger number. 

integer_comparison.sh

#!/bin/bash
VARIABLE_ONE="10"
VARIABLE_TWO="20"

if [[ "${VARIABLE_ONE}" -gt "{VARIABLE_TWO}" ]]; then
  echo "${VARIABLE_ONE} is greater than ${VARIABLE_TWO}."
else
  echo "${VARIABLE_ONE} is less than ${VARIABLE_TWO}."
fi

  We create two variables, VARIABLE_ONE and VARIABLE_TWO, and assign them values of 10 and 20 respectively. 
  We then use the -gt operator to compare the two values and print the result based on an integer comparison. (Page 31)


Linking Conditions 

  So far, we've used if to check whether a single condition is met. 
  But as with most programming languages, we can also use the OR (||) and AND(&&) operators to check for multiple conditions at once. 

  For example, what if we want to check that a file exists and that its size is greater than zero?

#!/bin/bash

echo "Hello World!" > file.txt

if [[ -f "file.txt" ]] && [[ -s "file.txt" ]]; then 
echo "The file exists and its size is greater than zero"

fi

  This code writes content to a file, then checks whether that file exists and whether its sizer is greater than zero. 
  Both conditions have to be met in order for the echo command to be executed. 
  If either returns false, nothing will happen. 

#!/bin/bash 
DIR_NAME="dir_test"

mkdir "${DIR_NAME}

if [[ -f "${DIR_NAME}" ]] || [[ -d "${DIR_NAME}" ]]; then 
echo "${DIR_NAME} is either a file or directory."
fi

  This code creates a directory then uses an if condition with the OR (||) operator to check whether the variable is a file (-f) or a directory (-d). 
  The second condition should evaluate to true, and the echo command should execute. 

Testing Command Success

  We can even test the exit code of commands to determine whether they were successful. 

if command; then
  # command was successful
fi

if ! command; then
  # command was unsuccessful
fi

  You'll often find yourself using this technique in bash, as comamnds aren't guaranteed to succeed. Failures could happen for reasons such as these
    A lack of the necessary permissions when creating resources
    An attempt to execute a command that is not available on the operating system
    This disk being full when downloading the file
    The network being down while executing network utilities

$ if touch test123; then
    echo "OK: file created"
fi

  We attempted to create a file. 
  Because the file creation succeeds, we print a message to indicate this. 

Checking subsequent conditions

  If the first if conditions fails, you can check for other conditions by using the elif keyword(short for else if). 
  To show how this works, we can write a program that checks the arguments passed to it on the command line. 


if_elif.sh 

#!/bin/bash
USER_INPUT="${1}"

if [[ -z "${USER_INPUT" }]]; then 
  echo "You must provide an argument!"
  exit 1
fi

if [[ -f ${USER_INPUT} ]]; then 
  echo "${USER_INPUT} is a file."
elif [[ -d "${USER_INPUT} ]]; then 
  echo "${USER_INPUT} is a directory"
else
  echo "${USER_INPUT} is not a file or directory."
fi
  
  We begin with an if statement that checks whether the variable USER_INPUT is null. (Page 33)
  This allows us to exit the script early by using exit 1 if we receive no command from the user. 

    if [[ -z "${USER_INPUT}" ]]; then
    echo "You must provide an argument!"
    exit 1
fi

  We then being a second if condition that uses the file test operator to check whether input is a file. 

if [[ -f "${USER_INPUT}" ]]; then 
  echo "${USER_INPUT} is a file."

  Below this condition we use elif to test whether the argument is a directory. 

if [[ -d "${USER_INPUT}" ]]; then 
  echo "${USER_INPUT} is a directory."
else

  This condition won't be tested unless the file fails. 
  If neither of these conditions is true, the script responds with the arugment netierh a file nor a directory. 

echo "${USER_INPUT} is not a file or a directory"
fi

