Loops and Loop Controls 

  Like many programming languages, bash lets you repeat chunks of code by using loops. 
  Loops can be particularly useful in your penetration adventures because they can help accomplish tasks such as the following: 

    Continously checking whether the IP address is online after a reboot until the IP address is reponsive. 
    Iterating through a list of hostnames (for example, to run a specific exploit against each of them or determine whether a firewall is protecting them)
    Testing for a certain condition and then running a loop when it is met (for example, checking whether a host is online and, if so, performing a brute-force attack against it)


The following secitons introduce you to the three kinds of loops in bash (while, until, and for) as well as the break and continue statements for working with loops. 

while (page 35)

  In bash, while loops allow you to run a code block until a test returns a successful exit status code. 
  You might use them in penetration testing to continously perform a port scan on a network and pick up any new hosts that join the network. 

Syntax of a while loop

while some_condition do
  # Run commands while the condition is true
done

  This loop starts with the keyword while, followed by an expression that describes the condition. 
  We then surround the code to be executed with the do and done keywords, which define the start and end of the code block. 

  You can use while loops to run a chunk of code infinitely by using true as the condition; because true always returns a successful exit code, the code will always run. 
  Let's use a while loop to repeatedly print a command to the screen. 

#/bin/bash

while true; do
  echo "Looping..."
  sleep 2
done

$ chmod u+x basic_while.sh
$ ./basic_while.sh

Next, let's write a more sophisticated while loop that runs until it finds a specific file ont he filesystem. 
Use CTRL-C to stop the code from executing at any point. 



while_loop.sh

#!/bin/bash
SIGNAL_TO_STOP_FILE="stoploop"

while[[ -f "${SIGNAL_TO_STOP_FILE}" ]]; do
  echo "The file ${SIGNAL_TO_STOP_FILE} does not exist yet..."
  echo "checking again in 2 seconds"
  sleep 2
done

echo "File was found! Exiting..."

  1. We define a variable representing the name of the file for whcih he while loops looks for using a file test operator.  

SIGNAL_TO_STOP_FILE="stoploop"

  2. The loop won't exit until the condition is satisfied. Once the file is available, the loop will stop, and the script will continue to the next command. 

while [[ -f "${SIGNAL_TO_STOP_FILE}" ]]; do 
  echo "The file ${SIGNAL_TO_STOP_FILE} does not yet exist..."
  echo "Checking again in 2 seconds"
done 

  3. The next command is an echo command that echos that the file has been found. 

echo "File was found! Exiting..."

$ chmod u+x while_loop.sh
$ ./while_loop.sh

  While the script is running, open a second terminal in the same directory as the script and create the stoploop file, 

touch stoploop

  Once you've done so, you should the see the script break out of the loop. 

  We can use while loops to monitor for filesystem events, such as file creations or deletions, or when a process starts. 
  This may comin in handy if an applications is suffering from a vulnerability that can only be temporarily abused. 

  For example, consider an application that runs daily at a particular hour and checks whether the file /tmp/update.sh exists; if it does, the application executes it as the root user. 
  Using a while loop, we can monitor when that application has started and then create the file just in time so our commands are executed by that application. 

until

  Whereas while runs so long as the condition succeeds, until runs so long as it fails. 

until some_condition do
  # Run some commands until the condition is no longer false
done

  Here is a script that uses until to run some commands until a file's size is greater than zero (meaning it is not empty). 

until_loops.sh

#!/bin/bash
FILE="output.txt"

touch "${FILE}"
until [[ -s "${FILE}" ]]; do
  echo "{FILE} is empty..."
  echo "Checking in again in 2 seconds..."
  sleep 2
done

echo "{FILE} appears to have some content in it!"

  We first create an empty file, then begin a loop that runs until the file is no longer empty.   
  Within the loop, we print messages to the terminal,. 
  Save this file as until_loop.sh and run it. 

$ chmod u+x until_loop.sh
$ ./until_loop.sh

  At this point, the script has created the file output.txt, but it's an empty file. (Page 38). 
  We can check this by using the du (disk usage) command. 

$ du -sb output.txt

  Open another terminal and navigate to the location at which your script is saved, then append some content to the file so its size is no longer zero. 

$ echo "until_loop_will_now_stop!" > output.txt

  The script should exit the loop, and you should see it print the following: 

output.txt appears to have some content in it. 

for 

  The for loop iterates over a sequence, such as a list of filenames or variables, or even a group of values generated by running a command. 
  Inside the for loop, we define a block of commands that are run against each value in the list, and each value in the list is assigned to a varaible name we define.

for variable_name in LIST; do
  # Run some commands for each item in the sequence.
done

  A simple way to use a for loop is to execute the same command multiple times. 

#!/bin/bas 

for index in $(seq 1 10); do
  echo "${index}"
done 

  A more practical example might use a for loop to run commands against a bunch of IP addresses passed on the command line. 
  The script underneath retrieves all arguments passed to the script, then iterates through tehma nd prints a message for each. 

#!/bin/bash

for ip_address in "$@"; do
  echo "Taking soem action on IP address ${ip_address}"
done 

  Save this script as for_loop_arguments.sh and run it as follows: 

$ chmod u+x for_loop_arguments.sh
$ ./for_loop_arguements.sh 10.0.0.1 10.0.0.2 192.168.1.1 192.168.1.2

  We can even run a loop on the output of commands such as ls. 
  In the script below, you can the names of all the files in the current working directory printed.

#!/bin/bash in $(ls .); do
  echo "File: ${file}"
done 

  We use a for loop to iterate over the output of .ls command, which lists the files in the current directory. 
  Each file will be assigned to the file variable as part of the for loop, so we can then use echo to print its name. 
  This technique would be useful, for example, if we wanted to perform an upload of all files in the directory, or even reanme them in bulk.

break and continue 

  Loops can run forever or until a condition is met. 
  But we can also exit a loop at any point by using the break keyword. 
  This keyword provides an alternative to the exit command, which would cause the entire script, not just the loop to eixt. 
  Using break, we can leave the loop and advance to the next code block. 

#!/bin/bash

while true; do
  echo "in the loop"
  break 
done 

echo "This code will be reached"

  in this case, the last echo command will be executed.
  The continue statement is used to jump to the next iteration of a loop. 
  We can use it to skip a certain value in a sequence. 

$ touch example_file1 example_file2 example_file3

#!/bin/bash

for file in example_file*; do
  if [[ "${file}" == "example_file1" ]]; then
    echo "Skipping the first file"
    continue
  fi

  echo "${RANDOM}" > "${file}"
done 

  1. We start a for loop with the example_file* glob, which will expand to match the names of all files starting with example_file in the directory where the script runs. 

for file in example_file*; do

  2. As a reuslt. the loop should iterate over all three files we created earlier. 
     Within the loop, we use string comparison to check whether the filename is equal to example_file1 because we want to skip this file and not make any changes to it. (page 40).
     If the conditions is met, we use the continue statement to process to the next iteration leaving the file unmodified. 
     Later in the loop, we use the echo command with the environment variable ${RANDOM} to generate a random number and write it into the file. 

if [[ "${file}" == "example_file1" ]]; then 
  echo "Skipping the next file"
  continue 
fi

  echo "${RANDOM}" > "${file}"
done

  Save this script as for_loop_continue.sh and execute it in the same directory as  the three files. 

$ chmod u+x for_loop_continue.sh
$ ./for_loop_continue.sh

  If you examine the files, you should see that the first file is empty, while the other two contain a random number as a result of the script echoing the value of the ${RANDOM} environment variable into them. 


case Statements

  In bash, case statements allow you to test multiple conditions in a cleaner way by using more readable syntax. 
  Often they help avoid many if conditions, which can become harder to read as they grow in size. 

case statement syntax

case EXPRESSION in 
  PATTERN1)
    # Do something if the first condition is met
;;
  PATTERN2)
    # Do something if the second condition is met. 
;;
esac

case_ip_address_check.sh 

#!/bin/bash
IP_ADDRESS="{1}"

case ${IP_ADDRESS} in 
  192.168.*)
    echo "Network is 192.168.x.x"
;;
  10.0.0.*)
    echo "Network is 10.0.x.x"
;;
*)
    echo "Could not identify the network"
;;
esac

  We define a variable that expects one command line argument to be passed (${1}) and saves it to the IP_ADDRESS variable. 
  We then use a pattern to check whether the IP_ADDRESS variable starts with 192.168. and a second pattern to check whether it starts with 10.0.
  We also define a default wildcard pattern using *, which returns a default message to the user if nothing else has matched.
  Save this file as case_ip_address_check.sh and ru it 

$ chmod u+x case_ip_address_check.sh
$ ./case_ip_address_check.sh 192.168.12.55
Network is 192.168.x.x

$ ./case_ip_address_check_sh 212.199.2.2
Could not identify

  A case statement can be used for a variety of cases. 
  For example, it can be used to run functions based on the input the user has entered. 
  Using case statements is a great way to handle the evaluation of multiple conditions without sacrificing the readability of code. 

Text Processing and Parsing 

  One of the most common things you'll find yourself doing in bash is processing text. 
  You can parse text on the command line by running one-off commands, or use a script to store parsed data in a variable that you can act on in some way. 
  Both approaches are important in many scenarios. 

Filtering with grep

  The grep command is one of the most popular Linux commands out there today. 
  We can use grep to filter out information of interest from streams. 
  At its most basic form:

$ grep "35.237.4.214" log.txt

  This grep command will read the file and extract any lines containing the IP addresses 35.237.4.214 from it. 
  We can use the grep for multiple patterns simultaneously. 
  The following backslash pipe (\|) acts as an or condition. 

$ grep "35.237.4.214"\|13.66.139.0" log.txt (Page 43). 



  
